1
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:06,200
Tiffany Ching at the Suffolk County Courthouse, where the trial of Oliver Martin

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,460
continued today. Martin, the noted jockey, was sworn in, then testified that he did

3
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:18,400
indeed throw the brisky handicap, but only because his horse, Bone Weary, told him

4
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:19,220
to do it.

5
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:25,680
The defense's star witness, psychiatrist Jason Siever, testified that Martin was

6
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:30,080
indeed insane. After his testimony, Dr.

7
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:31,740
Siever offered this comment.

8
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:34,480
Dr. Siever, do you think you can say...

9
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:34,660
No comment! No comment!

10
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:36,800
No comment! No comment!

11
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:38,980
Chrissy, Chrissy, Chrissy!

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,600
I can make Daddy walk backwards like a duck.

13
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,200
Chrissy, it's not a toy.

14
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:45,060
It's for grownups.

15
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:47,760
Yeah, right. And besides, I can make him cha -cha.

16
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:52,520
Do it again.

17
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:53,140
I want to see it.

18
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,680
Okay, okay. Watch this.

19
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:58,180
Oh, my poor baby.

20
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,400
I can't wait till those glasses of yours come in.

21
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:02,420
Oh, they're in the doctor called yesterday.

22
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,840
Actually, it was another doctor.

23
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:07,600
Um, Dr. Pepper.

24
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:11,420
Hello, everybody. Hi, honey.

25
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,420
Daddy, you're on TV!

26
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:16,240
Yes! Oh, Jason, we're all so proud of you.

27
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:18,140
Well, get ready to be even more proud.

28
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:21,060
Guess what I was offered because of all this trial publicity?

29
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:23,840
A column in the Long Island Sentinel.

30
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:25,320
Oh, hi. Yes.

31
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:26,160
That's great, Daddy.

32
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:27,880
What's a column?

33
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:32,340
Well, it's something you write that...

34
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:34,880
Well, sweetheart, it's kind of hard to explain if you haven't read a newspaper.

35
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:37,080
But some of us would still like to know.

36
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:42,780
Well, it's... Ben, it's a series of articles, all on one theme.

37
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:44,180
Mine will deal with psychology.

38
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:47,620
I'll help people with their fears and their hopes and their dreams and their

39
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:49,540
fantasy. Kind of like a 900 number.

40
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:52,560
What's a 900 number?

41
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,900
Well, it's kind of expensive, but, uh...

42
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:57,280
I have no idea.

43
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:01,340
I mean, I'd never pay $2 a minute to talk to some girl in a bikini.

44
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,120
Uh, hey, kids, let's go bake a hot apple pie.

45
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,260
Well, I think your column sounds great, Dad.

46
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:17,260
It's just too bad your other children aren't mature enough to appreciate it.

47
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:18,660
Ben, you moron!

48
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:20,040
You taped over Bambi!

49
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:23,380
Jason, tell me more about your column.

50
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:27,580
Well, I worked it all out over the phone with the editor, a guy named Doug Stanton.

51
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:31,700
Huh. Doug, if you give me the penny, I'll pinch it, Stanton.

52
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:33,420
Well, I didn't catch his middle name.

53
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:36,740
Oh, I worked for him years ago.

54
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:39,460
When it comes to paying writers, he can be frugal.

55
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:43,640
You gotta get up pretty early in the morning to frugal Jason Siever, Maggie.

56
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:46,300
If nothing else, I'm a good negotiator.

57
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:48,180
Oh, good. What's he paying?

58
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:49,920
Well, we haven't nailed that down yet.

59
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:51,380
How long is the column supposed to be?

60
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:53,040
Sometimes long, sometimes short.

61
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,100
It's just... Daily?

62
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:56,180
Weekly? It's unstructured, still.

63
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:57,800
Well, I assume you're getting violent.

64
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:00,160
Maggie, not all this is etched in stone.

65
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,320
So in other words, Mr.

66
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:06,180
Negotiator, you could be working long hours for little or no money while someone

67
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:06,840
else gets a credit?

68
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:09,200
I've been frugal.

69
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:13,180
Oh, show me that smile.

70
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:15,660
Oh, show me that smile.

71
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:19,560
Don't waste another minute on your crying.

72
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,460
We're nowhere near, near,

73
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:26,600
nowhere near. The best is ready to begin.

74
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:32,500
Oh, as long as we got each other,

75
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,920
we got the world spinning right in our hands.

76
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:46,060
You and me, we gotta be the luckiest dreamers We never quit dreaming As long as we

77
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:55,700
keep on giving We can take anything that comes our way Baby rain or shine, all the

78
00:03:55,700 --> 00:04:02,920
time We got each other sharing the laughter and love

79
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:32,900
I'm not going to be a good friend.

80
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:34,040
without a teleprompter.

81
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,300
So, what brings you here?

82
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:40,560
You wanna see people actually work for a living?

83
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:43,420
My husband's Jason Siever.

84
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:44,880
What, the Martin case?

85
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:46,720
The shrink with the big hair?

86
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:50,400
I hired him to do a column for us.

87
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:52,720
I'm here to make sure you don't take advantage of him.

88
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:55,620
Maggie, I'm insulted.

89
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:00,440
I'm giving him double basic with inflated top end.

90
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:03,000
Really? And how much do you pay the Paperboys?

91
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:06,060
Well, no top end.

92
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:11,540
Just after a decent salary and some guarantees on column length and byline.

93
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,460
Maggie, you're killing me.

94
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:16,400
Do you want him or not?

95
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:23,640
Okay. I'll pay him senior scale, but don't you dare say a word about this to any of

96
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:29,100
the Paperboys. I am gonna smash this worthless piece of junk.

97
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:32,960
You bought the shiitake 2000?

98
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:35,580
Why don't you just paint ink on your hand?

99
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:39,580
No. Here, hold this down while I thread this through the J -clips.

100
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:42,580
When did you learn so much about printers?

101
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,080
I researched them.

102
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:47,580
When I make a major purchase, I do my homework.

103
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:49,960
There, all better.

104
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:51,020
Now, wait a minute.

105
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:52,600
This could be a column.

106
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:56,400
Every week you could tell my readers how to get the best value for their dollar.

107
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:58,440
But I came here to talk about Jason's column.

108
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:00,180
Well, sure, but there's room for both of you.

109
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:01,360
He'll do the shrink stuff.

110
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:06,400
You do the microwaves, VCRs, furniture polish, all that Ralph Nader crud.

111
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:08,000
You mean consumer awareness?

112
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:09,980
See, you got a handle on this already.

113
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:12,260
Maggie, come on.

114
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:14,620
You're writing this punchy to the point.

115
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:20,220
I know you'll never use a passive sentence or a fancy buzzword like, uh, buzzword.

116
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:21,840
What do you say?

117
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:26,200
Will I receive some form of salary?

118
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:28,880
Maggie, you're killing me.

119
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:32,480
What are you doing, Mike?

120
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:35,300
Well, I'm making out my lesson plan for next week.

121
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:37,120
Oh, would you like to borrow Chrissy's crayons?

122
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:41,940
Ladies and gentlemen, ta -da.

123
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:48,840
One crack and I'm gone.

124
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:51,240
Pfft. Mike, cut that out.

125
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:53,780
Oh, you don't know what you're asking, Dad.

126
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:56,600
Well, I'll say something.

127
00:06:56,740 --> 00:06:58,000
I think you look brainy.

128
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:02,740
Aah! Oh, don't stop him, Dad.

129
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:04,260
My pancreas is about to blow.

130
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:07,520
Dad, brainy is just another word for geek.

131
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:08,560
Hey, I wear glasses.

132
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:09,340
I'm not a geek.

133
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:10,820
Sure, you've got a woman.

134
00:07:12,260 --> 00:07:13,840
Oh, man, I gotta go lay down.

135
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:15,100
I think I just hurt myself.

136
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:19,580
Hi, everybody. Hey.

137
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:22,260
Guess what? Your meeting went well with Doug Stanton?

138
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:23,860
Oh, did it ever.

139
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,340
And I got you.

140
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:27,080
Hold on to your hat.

141
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:30,100
More money than the paper boys.

142
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:35,960
Wow. And guess what I was offered?

143
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:38,060
Three bags full of waffle irons.

144
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:40,540
No. A column.

145
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:42,780
Honey, you're writing a column, too?

146
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:45,360
That's wonderful. Way to go, Mom.

147
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:49,100
I'm going to call it Maggie Malone Consumer Watchdog.

148
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:52,940
And I'm testing these for my first piece, so it's waffles all weekend.

149
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:57,140
Yes! And... Oh, I'll get it.

150
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:58,680
Well, isn't this exciting?

151
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:01,140
Two people in the same family with columns.

152
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:05,020
Daddy! Mommy gets waffles with her column.

153
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:06,440
What do you get?

154
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:08,720
Well, it's not a material thing.

155
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:11,940
It's just the satisfaction I get out of helping people solve their problems.

156
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:13,520
Oh, that's nice.

157
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:18,620
Boring. Well, I'm sorry, too.

158
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:27,020
Thank you. Well, I knew it was too good to be true.

159
00:08:27,260 --> 00:08:27,960
Who was that?

160
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:32,980
Doug Stanton. His publisher slashed his budget, so he only has enough money for one

161
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:36,780
column. Well, they better not cut your column, because they better not cut my

162
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:38,120
column. Who got the eggs here?

163
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:43,320
Neither. You know, he actually had the nerve to suggest that we each submit a sample

164
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,920
column. I told him that we refused to compete.

165
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:49,700
Honey, are you saying that the column is all yours.

166
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:52,120
Oh, that's one darn shame.

167
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:53,980
I'm sorry, Maggie.

168
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:55,180
I mean, are you all right with that?

169
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:56,620
Fine, I'm fine.

170
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:59,200
Carol, Chrissy, help me pack these stupid waffle irons.

171
00:08:59,200 --> 00:08:59,960
You said stupid.

172
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:01,820
You said st And that is a sign of tension.

173
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:02,660
I'm sensing you.

174
00:09:02,780 --> 00:09:03,820
Don't be silly.

175
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:05,440
I am very happy for you, Jason.

176
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:06,700
You sure? Absolutely.

177
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:12,640
Good. No, no, no, no, no, no, no.

178
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:14,280
Okay, Carol, Chrissy, stop packing.

179
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:17,840
Maggie, I am a professional at this, and I am sensing your pain here.

180
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:20,680
And if I let you give up your column, you would never forgive me.

181
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:22,960
Oh, so you're saying that you'll give up your column?

182
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:24,620
Just as I would never forgive you.

183
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:26,940
I think we ought to let Stanton decide.

184
00:09:27,220 --> 00:09:29,460
Jason, I don't think it's a good idea our competing.

185
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:31,720
Well, let's not think of this as competing, Maggie.

186
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:34,760
Let's see it as both of us having a chance at something wonderful.

187
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:37,200
Oh, what the heck.

188
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:40,400
Dessert waffles for everybody.

189
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:44,340
Yes! Somebody say waffles.

190
00:09:45,460 --> 00:09:48,220
Oh, Ben, I love your glasses.

191
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:50,200
They make you look so brainy.

192
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:52,720
That settles it.

193
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:53,880
I'm gonna die a virgin.

194
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:00,580
Am I in the right house?

195
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:03,500
Mike Siever studying twice in the same day?

196
00:10:05,140 --> 00:10:06,160
Come on, would you keep it down, Carol?

197
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:08,740
I happen to be working on something very technical and complex here.

198
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:10,760
Mike, have you finished the waffle questionnaire yet?

199
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:17,960
Yeah. Maggie, I've got a great title for my column.

200
00:10:18,540 --> 00:10:20,820
How about Between Your Ears with Jason Siever?

201
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:24,680
Sounds kind of crowded.

202
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:28,540
How about something punchy like shrink wrap?

203
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:30,860
No, no, seriously, how do you like mine?

204
00:10:31,880 --> 00:10:34,640
Well, Chrissy, come on, it sounds pretty bad.

205
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:40,780
Boy, Mom and Dad are flirting with big trouble.

206
00:10:41,400 --> 00:10:42,440
What are you talking about, Carol?

207
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:46,300
Mom and Dad both going after the same column?

208
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:49,800
Competition is a weed in the Garden of Love.

209
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:52,300
And you, Carol, are the fertilizer.

210
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:55,860
Oh, come on, Carol.

211
00:10:55,860 --> 00:10:57,680
What are you making such a big deal out of all this for?

212
00:10:57,960 --> 00:10:59,320
Because it is a big deal.

213
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,120
How can I explain it to you?

214
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:02,640
Let me use an example.

215
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:06,620
Okay, fine. All right, now suppose you and I were competing against each other for

216
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:09,640
something, okay? So there we are, brother and sister.

217
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:10,760
Then where are we competing at?

218
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:11,920
It doesn't matter.

219
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:13,220
It's an example, okay?

220
00:11:13,380 --> 00:11:14,360
So there we are.

221
00:11:14,420 --> 00:11:16,740
Now, how can I follow your example if I don't know what we're competing at?

222
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:19,540
Okay, fine. It's a spelling bee.

223
00:11:19,820 --> 00:11:20,960
Okay. Okay, fine.

224
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:23,120
Good. So there we are.

225
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:23,740
Now, wait a minute.

226
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:25,160
Why would I want to be in a spelling bee?

227
00:11:26,780 --> 00:11:27,880
It doesn't matter.

228
00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:29,500
It's a hypothetical situation.

229
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:30,960
But, Carol, you beat the pants off me.

230
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:32,460
Now, can't we just pick something I'd be good at?

231
00:11:32,760 --> 00:11:36,120
Okay, fine. How about one -on -one basketball?

232
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:38,000
Okay. Okay. All right.

233
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,840
Good. Basketball. So there we are.

234
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:42,720
Now why would you want to play basketball against me?

235
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:43,540
You can't even dribble.

236
00:11:44,420 --> 00:11:49,860
Ah! Mike, Carol, how does this sound from my first piece?

237
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:52,440
The id, ego, and superego,

238
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:56,040
merging the self in a non -fraternal world.

239
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:00,540
Ooh, I like that, Dad.

240
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:03,100
That's great. I think so.

241
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:03,580
I like it.

242
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:09,360
I'm excited. You're kidding.

243
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:10,700
You understood that?

244
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:14,240
Yeah. Dad's writing an article about fraternities.

245
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:21,520
Jason, is that a passive sentence?

246
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:23,660
Which one? That one.

247
00:12:24,760 --> 00:12:26,320
And that one.

248
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:30,340
And this one with the word buzzword in it?

249
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:31,180
Pretty good, huh?

250
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:36,220
I know it's funny how some people don't like that word.

251
00:12:37,160 --> 00:12:39,120
As a matter of fact, some people hate that word.

252
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:42,180
Mm. Jason? Mm -hmm?

253
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:43,540
Lose the word.

254
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:49,080
Honey, it's not like this is the first time I've ever been published.

255
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:51,060
Yes, but that was for other psychiatrists.

256
